---------------------------------------------------- WEB DEVELOPMENT ---------------------------------------------------------

Introduction to Web Development
 Web Development is the process of creating and maintaining websits and web pages

Types of Web development:

1.Frontend development
  Creates the user interface (UI)
  Everything the user can see and interact with

 Technologies:
 HTML
 CSS
 JavaScript

2.Backend Development
 Handles server-side logic
 Manages databases, authentication, and APIs

 Technologies:
 Node.js
 Python
 Java
 PHP
 MySQL
 MongoDB

3.Full Stack Development
 Works on both Frontend and Backend

What is a Web Browser?
 A web browser is software used to access and display websites.
Examples:
 Google Chrome
 Microsoft Edge
 Mozilla Firefox
 Safari

What is a Web Server?
A web server stores website files and sends them to users when requested.
Examples:
 Apache
 Nginx

Website:
 A collection of interconnected web pages

Webpage:
 A single page of a website

URL (Uniform Resource Locator):
 The address of a webpage

HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol):
 The protocol used for communication between the browser and the web server

HTTPS:
 A secure version of HTTP

HTML (HyperText Markup Language)
 HTML is the standard markup language used to create the structure of web pages
 HTML is not a programming languages
 It tells the browser how to display content on a webpages

What can HTML create?
 Headings
 Paragraphs
 Images
 Links
 Tables
 Lists
 Forms
 Buttons
 Videos
 Audio

HTML File Extension:
 HTML files are saved with the .html extension

Examples:
 index.html
 about.html
 contact.html
 
Basic HTML structur:
 Every HTML document follows a basic structure
Syntax:

 <!DOCTYPE html>
 <html>
 <head>
     <title> My website </title>
 </head>

 <body>
     
      hii

 </body>
 </html>

<!DOCTYPE html>
 Declares that the document is an HTML document
 It helps the browser render the webpage correctly

<html>
 The root element of an HTML document
 All HTML code is written inside this tag

<head>
 Contains information about the webpage
 This information is not displayed on the webpages

Examples:
 Title
 Meta tags
 CSS files
 JavaScript files

<title>
 Define the title of the webpage
 The title appears on the browser Tab

Example:
 <title> My website </title>

<body>
 Contains all the visible content of the webpage

Examples:
 Headings
 Paragraphs
 Images
 Links
 Tables
 Forms
 Lists

HTML tags
 HTML uses tags to define elements

 Most tags have:
 Opening tag
 <tag>
 closing tag
 </tag>

Example:
 <p>Hello World</p>

Nested tags:
 HTML tags can be placed inside other tags
Example:
    <html>
        <body>
            <h1>welcome</h1>
        </body>
    </html>

HTML tags:
 HTML provides 6 levels of heading to define titles and subtitles on a webpage

Syntax
 <h1>Heading 1</h1>   -> Largest and most important heading
 <h2>Heading 2</h2>   -> Second largest heading  
 <h3>Heading 3</h3>   -> Third largest heading
 <h4>Heading 4</h4>   -> Fourth largest heading
 <h5>Heading 5</h5>   -> Fifth largest  heading
 <h6>Heading 6</H6>   -> Smallest heading

Paragraph:
 The <p> tag is used to create a paragraph

Syntax:
 <p> paragraph </p>

Anchor Tag(Links):
 The <a> tag is used to create hyperlinks

Syntax:
 <a href="URL">Link Name</a>
 
href Attribute:
 The href attribute specifies the destination (URL) of the Link

Opening a Link in a New Tab:
 Use the target="_blank" attribute

Examples:
 <a href="https://www.google.com" target="_blank">Google</a>

HTML Attributes
 Attributes provide additional information about HTML elements

Syntax:
 <tag attribute="value">Content</tag>

Example:
 <a href="https://www.google.com">Google</a>

 href -> Attribute
 "https://www.google.com" -> Value

Line Break:
 The <br> tag moves the content to the next Line

Horizontal Line:
 The <hr> tag inserts a horizontal Line

HTML Comments
 Comments are not displayed on the webpages

Syntax:
 <!-- This is a comment -->

Image in HTML:
 The <img> tag is used to display an image on a webpage

Syntax:
 <img src ="image.jpg" alt="Image Description">

Attributes:
->arc(Source)
 Specifies the path of the image
  
->alt (Alternative Text)
 Displays text if the image cannot be loaded
 Improves accessibility

Examples:
 <img src="hello.jpg" alt="hello">

Image Width and Height:
 The width and height attributes are used to set the image size

Example:
 <img src="cat.jpg" a;t="cat" width="300" height="200">

Lists in HTML:
 HTML provides three types of Lists

1.Ordered List (<ol>)
  Display items in numbered Order

Example:
   <ol>
       <li>web</li>
       <li>banana</li> 
       <li>development</li>
    </ol>

2.Unordered List(<ul>)
  Display items using bullets

Example:
    <ul>
       <li>web</li>
       <li>mango</li>
       <li>development</li>
     </ul>

3.Description List(<dl>)
 Used to display terms and their descriptions

Tags Used:
 <dl> -> Description List
 <dt> -> Description term
 <dd> -> Description Definition

Example:
 <dl>
    <dt>HTML</dt>
    <dd>HyperText Markup Language</dd>

    <dt>CSS</dt>
    <dd>Cascading Style Sheets</dd>
  </dl>

Tables in HTML:
 The <Table> tag is used to create Tables

Structure:
  <table>
       <tr>
           <th>Name</th>
           <th>Age</th>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Abhishek</td>
            <td>20</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

Table Tags:

<Table> -> Define a table

<tr> -> Defines a table row

<th> -> Defines a table heading

<td> -> Defines table data (cell)

Colspan Attribute
 The colspan attribute allows a cell to span multiple columns

Example:
   <td colspan="2">Student Details</td>

rowspan Attribute
 The rowspan attribute allows a cell to span multiple rowspan

Example:
    <td rowspan="2">Abhishek</td>

SEO:
 SEO stands for Search Engine Optimization

 SEO is the process of improving a website so that it can rank better in search engine results

Why is SEO important?
 Improve website visibility
 Helps search engines understand the website
 Increases organic traffic
 Helps users find relevant content

Important SEO Practices
1.Title:
 The <title> tag defines the title of a webpage

2.Meta Description 
 Meta description provides a short description of the webpage

Example:
 <meta name="description" content="This is my personal website">

3.Headings
 HTML provides heading from <h1> to <h6>

 <h1>Main Heading</h1>
 <h2>Sub Heading</h2>
 <h3>Another Heading</h3>

 Headings should be used in a proper hierarchy

4.Semantic HTML
 Semantic HTML uses meaningful tags that describe the purpose of the content

Examples:
 <header>
 <nav>
 <main>
 <section>
 <article>
 <footer>

Coree Web Vitals:
 Core web vitals are metrics used to measure  important asppects of a webpage's user experience

There are three main Core Web Vitals:
1.LCP -> Largest Contentful Paint
 Measure how quickly the largest visible content loads
 Focus -> Loading Performance

2.INP ->Interaction to Next Paint
 Measures how quickly a webpage responds to user Interaction
 Focus -> Responsiveness

3.CLS -> Cumulative Layout Shift
 Meaasures unexpected movement of elements on a webpage while Loading 
 Focus -> Visual Stability

HTML Forms
 AA form  is used to collect input and information from users

Examples:
 Login Form
 Refistration Form
 Search Form
 Feedback Form

 The <form> tag is used to create a form

Basic Syntax:
 <form action="backend.php">

 </form> -> Action specifies where the form data should be sent for processing

Input Tag:
 The <input>  tag is used to create interactive input fields in a form

Syntax:
 <input type="text">

The type attribute specifies the type of input

Common Input Type:
 text
 email
 password
 number
 date 
 radio
 checkbox
 submit

Examples:
 <input type="text">
 <input type="email">
 <input type="password">
 <input type="number">
 <input type="date">

Name Attribute:
 The name attribute is used to identify the input field when the form data is submitted

Example:
 <input type="text" name="username">

GET:
 Sends data through the URL
 Data can be visible in the URL
 Suitable for non-sensitive data

Example:
 <form action="backend.php" method="get">

POST:
 Sends data in the request body
 Data is not displayed in the URL
 Commonly used for submitting sensitive or large amounts of data

Example:
 <form action="backend.php" method="post">

Label Tag:
 The <label> tag defines a label for an input field

Example:
 <label for="name">Name:</label>
 <input type="text" id="name" name="name">

 The for attribute of <label> should match the id of the input

Textarea:
 The <textarea> tag is used to create a multi-line text input field

Example:
 <textarea name="message" rows="5" cols="30">
 </textarea>

it is usedful for:
 Comments
 Feedback
 Messages
 Addresses

Submit Buttom
 The submit input is used to submit the form

Example:
 <input type="submit" value="Submit">

Example:
 <form action="backend.php" method="post">
    
     <label for="name">Name:</label>
     <input type="text" id="name" name="name">

     <br><br>

     <label for="email">Email:</label>
     <input type="email" id="email" name="email">

     <br><br>

     label for="message">Message:</label>
     <textarea id="message" name="message"></textarea>

     <br><br>

     <input type="submit" value="Submit">

 </form>

INLINE AND BLOCK ELEMENTS IN HTML:
Block-Level Elements
 A block-level elements always starts on a new line and takes up the full available width
 Take full availabble width

Inline Elements:
 An inline element does not start on a new line
 It takes only as much width as necessary

Block Elements (Most Commonly Used First)
 <div>: A generic container for flow content.
 <p>: Paragraph.
 <h1>, <h2>, <h3>, <h4>, <h5>, <h6>: Headings.
 <ul>: Unordered list.
 <ol>: Ordered list.
 <li>: List item.
 <form>: A section containing form controls.
 <table>: Table.
 <section>: A standalone section of a document.
 <header>: A container for introductory content or a set of navigational links.
 <footer>: Footer of a section or page.
 <nav>: A section of a page that contains navigation links.
 <article>: A self-contained composition in a document.
 <aside>: A section of a page that contains information indirectly related to the main content.
 <main>: The main content of a document.
 <fieldset>: A set of form controls grouped under a common name.
 <blockquote>: A block of text that is a quotation from another source.
 <pre>: Preformatted text.
 <canvas>: A container used to draw graphics via JavaScript.
 <dl>: Description list.
 <dt>: Term in a description list.
 <dd>: Description in a description list.
 <figure>: Any content that is referenced from the main content.
 <figcaption>: A caption for a <figure> element.
 <address>: Contact information for the author or owner of the document.
 <hr>: A thematic break or a horizontal rule.
 <tfoot>: Footer of a table.

Inline Elements (Most Commonly Used First)
 <a>: Anchor or hyperlink.
 <img>: Image.
 <span>: Generic inline container.
 <input>: Input field.
 <label>: Label for a form element.
 <strong>: Strong emphasis.
 <em>: Emphasized text.
 <br>: Line break.
 <code>: Code snippet.
 <b>: Bold text.
 <i>: Italic text.
 <u>: Underlined text.
 <small>: Smaller text.
 <sub>: Subscript.
 <sup>: Superscript.
 <mark>: Marked or highlighted text.
 <q>: Short inline quotation.
 <cite>: Citation.
 <kbd>: Keyboard input.
 <samp>: Sample output.
 <var>: Variable in a mathematical expression or programming context.
 <time>: Time.
 <abbr>: Abbreviation.
 <data>: Machine-readable translation of content.
 <acronym>: Acronym (Not supported in HTML5). 




    













